\fBvect\fR\fR\&[\fB\&=\fR\fIoption\fI\fR\&[\fB\&,\fR\fIoption\fI\fR\
\&...]] 
.br
Perform vectorization. If no \fR\fIoption\fI\fRs are specified, then \
all vector optimizations are enabled. The available \fR\fIoption\fI\fR\
s are:
.RE
.ta 0.50i +0.5i +0.5i +0.5i +0.5i
.RS 2.4i
.LP
\fBcachesize:\fR\fInumber\fI\fR\(emThis sets the size of the portion of \
the cache used by the vectorizer to \fR\fInumber\fI\fR\& bytes. \fR\fI\
number\fI\fR\& must be a multiple of 64, and less than 8192. \
Normally the best results are obtained using a cache size of 4096. 
.RE
.RS 2.4i
.LP
\fBnoassoc\fR\fR\(emWhen scalar reductions are present (for example, dot product), and loop unrolling is turned on, \
the compiler may change the order of operations so that it can generate better code. This transformation \
can change the result of the computation due to round-off error. The use of \fB\
noassoc\fR\fR\& prevents this transformation.
.RE
.RS 2.4i
.LP
\fBrecog\fR\fR\(emRecognize certain loops as simple vector loops and call a special routine.\
\& 
.RE
.RS 2.4i
.LP
\fBsmallvect\fR\fR\&[\fB\&:\fR\fInumber\fI\fR\&]\(emThis option allows the \
vectorizer to assume that the maximum vector length is no greater than \fR\fI\
number\fI\fR\&. \fR\fInumber\fI\fR\& must be a multiple of 10. Values greater than 1270 should not be used. If \fR\fI\
number\fI\fR\& is not specified, the value 100 is used. This \
option allows the vectorizer to avoid stripmining in cases where it cannot determine the maximum vector \
length. In doubly-nested, non-perfectly nested loops, this option can allow invariant vector motion that \
would not otherwise have been possible. Incorrect code will result if this option is used, and a vector takes \
on a length greater than specified. 
.RE
.RS 2.4i
.LP
\fBtransform\fR\fR\(emPerform high-level transformations such \
